insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0223$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Comparing Enthalpy Changes$q$, $q$Compare the magnitude of heat evolved between two exothermic reactions from their ΔH values.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "6i2n9s", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 65, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Reaction (1) releases LESS heat than reaction (2) (286 kJ vs 406 kJ), not more — this reverses the comparison.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Both ΔH values are negative, meaning both reactions RELEASE heat — neither one absorbs heat, so this option misreads the sign entirely.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "ΔH describes the energy change of a reaction, not how fast it proceeds — reaction rate can't be read off an enthalpy value at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Same issue as D — ΔH gives no information about reaction speed, only about the size of the energy change.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the reactions (1) H2(g) + 1/2O2(g) → H2O(l); ΔH = -286kJ, and (2) C(s) + O2(g) → CO2(g); ΔH = -406kJ, the equations imply that$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "more heat is evolved in (1)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "more heat is absorbed in (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "less heat is evolved in (1)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "reaction (2) proceeds faster than (1)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "reaction (1) proceeds faster than (2)", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Both reactions release heat (the negative sign tells you that — neither one "absorbs" anything), so compare the sizes: reaction 1 releases 286 kJ, reaction 2 releases 406 kJ. Reaction 1 releases less. That's all this question is asking — comparing magnitudes, not rates. ΔH tells you nothing about how fast a reaction goes, so any option about speed can be ruled out immediately.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Reaction (1) releases LESS heat than reaction (2) (286 kJ vs 406 kJ), not more — this reverses the comparison. B: Both ΔH values are negative, meaning both reactions RELEASE heat — neither one absorbs heat, so this option misreads the sign entirely. D: ΔH describes the energy change of a reaction, not how fast it proceeds — reaction rate can't be read off an enthalpy value at all. E: Same issue as D — ΔH gives no information about reaction speed, only about the size of the energy change.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0224$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Reactivity and Electrochemical Series$q$, $q$Determine which of four metals dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid, accounting for a passivating exception.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6wz62", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 60, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Copper sits below hydrogen in the reactivity series and simply does not react with dilute HCl — it can't be included.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Copper sits below hydrogen and doesn't dissolve in dilute HCl at all — this incorrectly includes it.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Lead is above hydrogen in reactivity but forms an insoluble PbCl2 coating that halts the reaction almost immediately — the general reactivity-series rule alone misses this practical exception.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Fe also sits above hydrogen and dissolves in dilute HCl just like Mg — leaving it out understates which metals react.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of these metals, Mg, Fe, Pb, and Cu will dissolve in dilute HCl?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "All the metals", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Mg, Fe, and Cu", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mg, Fe, and Pb", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Mg and Fe only", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "Mg only", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Run each metal against the reactivity series relative to hydrogen. Mg and Fe both sit above hydrogen, so both displace it from the acid and dissolve. Copper sits below hydrogen and simply doesn't react with dilute acids. Lead is technically above hydrogen but forms an insoluble PbCl2 coating almost immediately, which shuts the reaction down — so in practice it doesn't dissolve either. That's the kind of exception worth remembering by name, not just by the general rule.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Copper sits below hydrogen in the reactivity series and simply does not react with dilute HCl — it can't be included. B: Copper sits below hydrogen and doesn't dissolve in dilute HCl at all — this incorrectly includes it. C: Lead is above hydrogen in reactivity but forms an insoluble PbCl2 coating that halts the reaction almost immediately — the general reactivity-series rule alone misses this practical exception. E: Fe also sits above hydrogen and dissolves in dilute HCl just like Mg — leaving it out understates which metals react.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0225$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Alloys$q$, $q$Recall the composition of stainless steel.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4ihrpp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Lead has no role in stainless steel's rust resistance — a distractor built from a plausible-sounding but wrong third metal.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Copper is not the ingredient responsible for stainless steel's corrosion resistance — chromium is.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Silver has no role in stainless steel's composition — another plausible-sounding wrong metal.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Carbon and iron alone make ordinary (non-stainless) steel, which does corrode — the chromium that prevents rusting is missing from this option.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Stainless steel is an alloy of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Carbon, iron and lead", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Carbon, iron and chromium", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Carbon, iron and copper", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Carbon, iron and silver", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Carbon and iron only", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Chromium is the ingredient that makes stainless steel "stainless" — it forms a thin protective oxide layer that resists rusting. Carbon and iron alone just give you ordinary steel, which corrodes.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Lead has no role in stainless steel's rust resistance — a distractor built from a plausible-sounding but wrong third metal. C: Copper is not the ingredient responsible for stainless steel's corrosion resistance — chromium is. D: Silver has no role in stainless steel's composition — another plausible-sounding wrong metal. E: Carbon and iron alone make ordinary (non-stainless) steel, which does corrode — the chromium that prevents rusting is missing from this option.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0226$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Titration Calculations$q$, $q$Compute the volume of a diprotic acid needed to exactly neutralize a given base solution.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6pk77", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 55, $q$low$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This treats the acid-to-base mole ratio as 1:1 instead of the real 1:2, giving a volume 2.5 times too large.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This comes from an arithmetic slip while converting moles of NaOH to moles of H2SO4 or to a final volume.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Another arithmetic slip along the same calculation chain, landing on a volume larger than the correct 2.0 cm3.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "This overshoots even further — consistent with skipping the ÷2 mole-ratio step and also misplacing a decimal.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What volume of 0.50 M H2SO4 will exactly neutralize 20cm3 of 0.1 M NaOH solution?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2.0 cm3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "5.0 cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "6.8 cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "8.3 cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "10.4 cm3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$First write the balanced equation: H2SO4 + 2NaOH → Na2SO4 + 2H2O — notice it's 1 mole of acid to 2 moles of base, not 1:1. Moles of NaOH = 0.020 dm3 × 0.1 = 0.002 mol. That needs 0.002/2 = 0.001 mol H2SO4. Volume = 0.001/0.50 = 0.002 dm3 = 2.0 cm3. The step students skip is checking the mole ratio from the equation before dividing — H2SO4 is diprotic, so it always needs half as many moles as the base it's neutralizing.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This treats the acid-to-base mole ratio as 1:1 instead of the real 1:2, giving a volume 2.5 times too large. C: This comes from an arithmetic slip while converting moles of NaOH to moles of H2SO4 or to a final volume. D: Another arithmetic slip along the same calculation chain, landing on a volume larger than the correct 2.0 cm3. E: This overshoots even further — consistent with skipping the ÷2 mole-ratio step and also misplacing a decimal.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0227$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Oxidation States of Common Gases$q$, $q$Identify which pair of gases is already at a stable oxidation state and won't react further with oxygen.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ww2ejp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 75, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "SO2 can still be oxidized further to SO3, and NH3 can still be oxidized to NO — both gases in this pair can react further.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "CO2 is already stable, but H2 in the same pair will still burn readily with oxygen — only checking one gas in the pair misses this.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "SO3 is stable here, but NO can still be oxidized further to NO2 — the pair isn't fully unreactive.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "CO and H2 are both classic combustible gases that react readily with oxygen — neither is unreactive here.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following pairs of gases will NOT react further with oxygen at a temperature between 30°C and 400°C?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "SO2 and NH3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CO2 and H2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "NO2 and SO3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "SO3 and NO", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "CO and H2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Ask, for each gas, "has this already been oxidized as far as it easily goes?" SO2 and NH3 can both still be oxidized further (to SO3 and NO respectively). CO2 can't react further, but H2 in the same pair absolutely will burn with oxygen. NO can still oxidize to NO2. CO and H2 are both classic combustible gases that react readily with O2. NO2 and SO3, though, are both already at their common stable oxidation states under these mild conditions — neither reacts further. Check every gas in the pair, not just one.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: SO2 can still be oxidized further to SO3, and NH3 can still be oxidized to NO — both gases in this pair can react further. B: CO2 is already stable, but H2 in the same pair will still burn readily with oxygen — only checking one gas in the pair misses this. D: SO3 is stable here, but NO can still be oxidized further to NO2 — the pair isn't fully unreactive. E: CO and H2 are both classic combustible gases that react readily with oxygen — neither is unreactive here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0228$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Salt Preparation$q$, $q$Spot the step that uses the wrong acid in a multi-step salt preparation procedure.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "liww8z", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 70, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Concentrating the solution by evaporation is a genuinely correct step in crystallizing a soluble salt — the real flaw is earlier, in choosing the wrong acid.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Cooling and filtering out crystals is the correct crystallization technique — this step is not the flaw.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Washing crystals with a small amount of very cold water (to remove surface impurities without dissolving much of the product) is standard, correct practice.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Air-drying crystals is a correct final step — the flaw is not here, it's in which acid was used at the very start.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the preparation of some pure crystals of Cu(NO3)2 starting with CuO, a student gave the following statements as steps he employed. Which of these shows a flaw in his report? A) Some CuO was reacted with excess dilute H2SO4; B) The solution was concentrated; C) When the concentrate was cooled, crystals formed were removed by filtration; D) The crystals were washed with very cold water; E) The crystals were then allowed to dry.$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Some CuO was reacted with excess dilute H2SO4", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "The solution was concentrated", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "When the concentrate was cooled, crystals formed were removed by filtration", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "The crystals were washed with very cold water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "The crystals were then allowed to dry", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Before checking the method, check the chemistry matches the target. The student is trying to make copper (II) nitrate, Cu(NO3)2 — but step A reacts CuO with sulphuric acid, which would give copper sulphate instead. Wrong acid, wrong product, no matter how correct every step after it is. It's worth reading a multi-step procedure question by first asking "does this even lead to the right compound?" before checking the technique.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Concentrating the solution by evaporation is a genuinely correct step in crystallizing a soluble salt — the real flaw is earlier, in choosing the wrong acid. C: Cooling and filtering out crystals is the correct crystallization technique — this step is not the flaw. D: Washing crystals with a small amount of very cold water (to remove surface impurities without dissolving much of the product) is standard, correct practice. E: Air-drying crystals is a correct final step — the flaw is not here, it's in which acid was used at the very start.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0229$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Fermentation and Distillation$q$, $q$Identify the separation process that overcomes the ethanol-water azeotrope to give high-purity ethanol.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kk9o6y", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 55, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Fractional distillation alone still runs into the ethanol-water azeotrope ceiling around 95-96% — without a dehydrant it can't push purity any higher.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Simple distillation gives an even poorer separation than fractional distillation, and still hits the same azeotrope ceiling without a dehydrant.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Column chromatography isn't the practical, standard method for concentrating ethanol from a fermented liquid — distillation is.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Evaporation doesn't separate ethanol from water at all cleanly — both are volatile, so it doesn't purify the ethanol.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following separation processes is most likely to yield high quality ethanol (>95%) from palm wine?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Fractional distillation without a dehydrant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Simple distillation without a dehydrant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fractional distillation with a dehydrant", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Column chromatography", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Evaporation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Ethanol and water form an azeotrope around 95-96%, which means ordinary distillation — however careful — physically can't push the purity higher than that. To get past that ceiling, you need a dehydrating agent to pull out the remaining water chemically, alongside fractional distillation to separate the components as cleanly as possible in the first place.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Fractional distillation alone still runs into the ethanol-water azeotrope ceiling around 95-96% — without a dehydrant it can't push purity any higher. B: Simple distillation gives an even poorer separation than fractional distillation, and still hits the same azeotrope ceiling without a dehydrant. D: Column chromatography isn't the practical, standard method for concentrating ethanol from a fermented liquid — distillation is. E: Evaporation doesn't separate ethanol from water at all cleanly — both are volatile, so it doesn't purify the ethanol.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0230$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Kinetic Theory & Gas Laws$q$, $q$Gas Laws$q$, $q$State the effect of increasing pressure on gas density at constant temperature.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "84csd7", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 30, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Kinetic energy depends on temperature, not pressure — pressure alone (at constant temperature) doesn't change the molecules' average kinetic energy.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Compressing a gas into a smaller volume increases its density (same mass, less space) — this states the opposite effect.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Pressure and temperature are independent variables here — increasing pressure at constant temperature doesn't itself cool the gas.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Increasing pressure compresses a gas into a SMALLER volume, not a larger one — this states the opposite effect.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Increasing the pressure of a gas$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "lowers the average kinetic energy of the molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "decreases the density of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "decreases the temperature of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "increases the density of the gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "increases the volume of the gas", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Squeeze a gas into a smaller volume (that's what increasing pressure does, at constant temperature) and the same mass of gas is now packed into less space — density is mass over volume, so it goes up. Kinetic energy depends on temperature, not pressure, so options touching temperature or kinetic energy are distractors mixing up two separate gas properties.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Kinetic energy depends on temperature, not pressure — pressure alone (at constant temperature) doesn't change the molecules' average kinetic energy. B: Compressing a gas into a smaller volume increases its density (same mass, less space) — this states the opposite effect. C: Pressure and temperature are independent variables here — increasing pressure at constant temperature doesn't itself cool the gas. E: Increasing pressure compresses a gas into a SMALLER volume, not a larger one — this states the opposite effect.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0231$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Combustion Calculations$q$, $q$Use a balanced combustion equation to compute the net mass gain from trapping the water product.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "l5d6y3", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 4, $q$application$q$, 95, $q$high$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This undershoots the real water mass produced (5,400 kg) — likely a slip in scaling the balanced equation up to 3,000 kg of ethane.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is much smaller than the actual mass of water retained — consistent with a major scaling error in the mole-ratio calculation.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Another undershoot from the same kind of scaling slip in converting moles of water to kilograms.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "This is exactly half of the correct 2,400 kg — consistent with a factor-of-2 error somewhere in the mole ratio (e.g. using 3 mol H2O per mol C2H6 instead of the correct 3 mol per mol from the balanced equation).", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A jet plane carrying 3,000 kg of ethane burns off all the gas forming water and carbon dioxide. If all the carbon dioxide is expelled and the water formed is condensed and kept on board the plane, then the gain in weight is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "1,800kg", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "900kg", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "600kg", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "2,400 kg", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "1,200kg", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$The extra weight comes from oxygen pulled in from the surrounding air and locked into the water that stays on board — the CO2 carrying its share of that oxygen simply leaves. Balanced equation: 2C2H6 + 7O2 → 4CO2 + 6H2O. Scaling 2 mol C2H6 (60g) up to 3,000 kg is a factor of 50. Water produced: 6 mol × 18 × 50 = 5,400 kg. "Gain in weight" is comparing what's kept (the water) against what you started with (the ethane): 5,400 - 3,000 = 2,400 kg. The key idea: the plane gains weight because it's absorbing oxygen from the air and trapping it as water, not because ethane itself got heavier.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This undershoots the real water mass produced (5,400 kg) — likely a slip in scaling the balanced equation up to 3,000 kg of ethane. B: This is much smaller than the actual mass of water retained — consistent with a major scaling error in the mole-ratio calculation. C: Another undershoot from the same kind of scaling slip in converting moles of water to kilograms. E: This is exactly half of the correct 2,400 kg — consistent with a factor-of-2 error somewhere in the mole ratio (e.g. using 3 mol H2O per mol C2H6 instead of the correct 3 mol per mol from the balanced equation).$q$),
($q$chemistry_0232$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Identifying Acids and Gas Tests$q$, $q$Identify a liquid from its reaction with a carbonate, using the standard carbonate/acid gas test.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vpzbrw", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Na2SO4 is a neutral salt solution — it doesn't react with Na2CO3 to release CO2 at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "KI is a neutral salt solution too — no reaction with Na2CO3 that would release CO2.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "An alkali reacting with a carbonate solution doesn't release CO2 gas — that reaction specifically requires an acid attacking the carbonate.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "A hydrocarbon is non-reactive with a carbonate solution under these conditions — it has no acidic proton to attack the carbonate with.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Liquid X reacts with sodium trioxocarbonate (IV) (Na2CO3) to give a gas which turns lime water milky. X is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Na2SO4 (aq)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "KI (aq)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "An alkali", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "An acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "A hydrocarbon", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Any carbonate releasing CO2 (the gas that turns limewater milky) has been attacked by an acid — that's the standard test for a carbonate, run in reverse to identify what X is. Neither a salt, an alkali, nor a hydrocarbon reacts with Na2CO3 this way.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Na2SO4 is a neutral salt solution — it doesn't react with Na2CO3 to release CO2 at all. B: KI is a neutral salt solution too — no reaction with Na2CO3 that would release CO2. C: An alkali reacting with a carbonate solution doesn't release CO2 gas — that reaction specifically requires an acid attacking the carbonate. E: A hydrocarbon is non-reactive with a carbonate solution under these conditions — it has no acidic proton to attack the carbonate with.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0233$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$General Reactivity Facts$q$, $q$Identify the false statement among five general chemistry facts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vfmsto", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$analysis$q$, 55, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Zinc can reduce Cu2+ to Cu+ (and further to Cu metal) in the presence of HCl — this statement is chemically accurate, so it's not the false one.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Nitrogen gas genuinely has very low solubility in water — this statement is true, not the false one being asked for.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Carbon dioxide is genuinely soluble in water (forming carbonic acid) — this statement is true, not the false one.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Lead's atomic weight (~207) is indeed higher than copper's (~63.5) — this statement is true, not the false one.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following statements is FALSE? A) Copper (II) ion can be reduced to copper (I) ion by hydrochloric acid and zinc; B) Sodium metal dissolves in water giving oxygen; C) Nitrogen is insoluble in water; D) Carbon dioxide is soluble in water; E) Lead has a higher atomic weight than copper$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Copper (II) ion can be reduced to copper (I) ion by hydrochloric acid and zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sodium metal dissolves in water giving oxygen", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Nitrogen is insoluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Carbon dioxide is soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Lead has a higher atomic weight than copper", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$On "which statement is false" questions, check each one against something you're certain of rather than guessing which one "feels" wrong. Sodium reacting with water is a reaction you should know cold: 2Na + 2H2O → 2NaOH + H2 — it releases hydrogen, not oxygen. That's a clear, checkable error, which is exactly what makes it the false statement here.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Zinc can reduce Cu2+ to Cu+ (and further to Cu metal) in the presence of HCl — this statement is chemically accurate, so it's not the false one. C: Nitrogen gas genuinely has very low solubility in water — this statement is true, not the false one being asked for. D: Carbon dioxide is genuinely soluble in water (forming carbonic acid) — this statement is true, not the false one. E: Lead's atomic weight (~207) is indeed higher than copper's (~63.5) — this statement is true, not the false one.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0234$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Enthalpy of Dissolution$q$, $q$Recall whether dissolving sodium nitrite in water is exothermic or endothermic.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "fiv6k4", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$recall$q$, 40, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is the opposite of NaNO2's real dissolution behaviour — its lattice energy outweighs the hydration energy released, making the process draw heat IN, not release it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "'Isothermic' would mean no net heat change at all — but NaNO2 dissolving genuinely draws in heat and measurably cools its surroundings.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'Isomeric' describes molecules with the same formula but different structure — an unrelated term used as a plausible-sounding distractor here.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "'Hygroscopic' describes a solid absorbing moisture from the air, not the heat behaviour of a solid dissolving in water — a different property entirely.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When sodium dioxonitrate (III) (NaNO2) dissolves in water, the process is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Exothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Endothermic", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Isothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Isomeric", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Hygroscopic", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$This is a specific factual recall — for NaNO2, the lattice energy holding the ionic solid together outweighs the hydration energy released when the ions are surrounded by water, so the solution has to draw heat in from its surroundings to dissolve. That net energy deficit is what makes a dissolution endothermic rather than exothermic.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is the opposite of NaNO2's real dissolution behaviour — its lattice energy outweighs the hydration energy released, making the process draw heat IN, not release it. C: 'Isothermic' would mean no net heat change at all — but NaNO2 dissolving genuinely draws in heat and measurably cools its surroundings. D: 'Isomeric' describes molecules with the same formula but different structure — an unrelated term used as a plausible-sounding distractor here. E: 'Hygroscopic' describes a solid absorbing moisture from the air, not the heat behaviour of a solid dissolving in water — a different property entirely.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0235$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Rates of Reaction$q$, $q$Rate of Reaction — Surface Area$q$, $q$Identify which change most increases reaction rate via surface area.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "5mzsr0", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Using a greater volume of acid (at the same concentration) changes how much acid is available in total, not the surface area of zinc exposed at any moment — it doesn't meaningfully speed up the rate.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "A smaller volume of acid, if anything, would reduce the reaction's extent, not increase its rate.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "An ice-bath lowers temperature, which SLOWS a reaction down — the opposite of what the question is asking for.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Pellets have much LESS surface area than powdered zinc, for the same mass — this would slow the reaction relative to powder, not speed it up.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2. The rate of the above reaction will be greatly increased if$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "the zinc is in the powdered form", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "a greater volume of the acid is used", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "a smaller volume of the acid is used", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "the reaction vessel is immersed in an ice-bath", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "the zinc is in the form of pellets", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Powdering the zinc massively increases its surface area exposed to the acid, which is the single biggest rate booster among the options here — more surface means more collisions happening at once. Volume of acid changes total amount available, not the rate at which it reacts. An ice-bath would slow the reaction down, the opposite of what's being asked.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Using a greater volume of acid (at the same concentration) changes how much acid is available in total, not the surface area of zinc exposed at any moment — it doesn't meaningfully speed up the rate. C: A smaller volume of acid, if anything, would reduce the reaction's extent, not increase its rate. D: An ice-bath lowers temperature, which SLOWS a reaction down — the opposite of what the question is asking for. E: Pellets have much LESS surface area than powdered zinc, for the same mass — this would slow the reaction relative to powder, not speed it up.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0236$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Limiting Reagent$q$, $q$Determine the mass of zinc left unreacted after identifying the limiting reagent.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "sam5eu", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 75, $q$medium$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This doesn't match either the mass of zinc reacted (0.65g) or the mass left over (1.35g) — likely a slip in the final subtraction step.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is close to the correct leftover mass but off by a rounding or subtraction slip.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "0.65g is the mass of zinc that REACTED, not the mass left over — this option answers a different part of the calculation than what was asked.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "This is far too small to be either the reacted or leftover mass — consistent with a major unit or mole-ratio error.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2. In the above reaction how much zinc will be left undissolved if 2.00 g of zinc is treated with 10cm3 of 1.0 M of H2SO4? [Zn=65, S=32, O=16, H=1]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "1.35g", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "1.00 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "0.70g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "0.65 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "0.06g", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Don't rush into the numbers — first work out which reactant actually runs out. Moles of Zn available = 2.00/65 ≈ 0.0308 mol. Moles of H2SO4 available = 0.010 dm3 × 1.0 = 0.010 mol. The equation is 1:1, so only 0.010 mol of Zn can actually react — the acid runs out first and limits the reaction. Mass of Zn used = 0.010 × 65 = 0.65g. Zinc left over = 2.00 - 0.65 = 1.35g.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This doesn't match either the mass of zinc reacted (0.65g) or the mass left over (1.35g) — likely a slip in the final subtraction step. C: This is close to the correct leftover mass but off by a rounding or subtraction slip. D: 0.65g is the mass of zinc that REACTED, not the mass left over — this option answers a different part of the calculation than what was asked. E: This is far too small to be either the reacted or leftover mass — consistent with a major unit or mole-ratio error.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0237$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Limiting Reagent$q$, $q$Determine which reagent is in excess in a precipitation reaction and by what volume.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "sam5eu", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 80, $q$medium$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This overstates the excess volume — an arithmetic slip somewhere after correctly identifying NaOH as the excess reagent.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Also overstates the excess volume compared to the correct 40cm3 — a slip in the subtraction or division step.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Al(NO3)3 is not the excess reagent here — NaOH is present in more than the 3:1 ratio it needs, so Al(NO3)3 is actually the limiting reagent, not the one in excess.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Same issue as D — this misidentifies which reagent is in excess; it's NaOH, not Al(NO3)3.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$30cm3 of 0.1 M Al(NO3)3 solution is reacted with 100cm3 of 0.15M NaOH solution. Which is in excess and by how much?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "NaOH solution, by 70cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "NaOH solution, by 60cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "NaOH solution by 40cm3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Al(NO3)3 solution by 20cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Al(NO3)3 solution, by 10cm3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Equation: Al(NO3)3 + 3NaOH → Al(OH)3 + 3NaNO3 — three moles of NaOH for every mole of aluminium nitrate. Moles of Al(NO3)3 = 0.030 × 0.1 = 0.003 mol, which needs 3 × 0.003 = 0.009 mol NaOH. Moles of NaOH actually present = 0.100 × 0.15 = 0.015 mol. That's 0.015 - 0.009 = 0.006 mol more than needed. Converting that excess back to a volume at 0.15 M: 0.006/0.15 = 0.04 dm3 = 40cm3 of NaOH left over.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This overstates the excess volume — an arithmetic slip somewhere after correctly identifying NaOH as the excess reagent. B: Also overstates the excess volume compared to the correct 40cm3 — a slip in the subtraction or division step. D: Al(NO3)3 is not the excess reagent here — NaOH is present in more than the 3:1 ratio it needs, so Al(NO3)3 is actually the limiting reagent, not the one in excess. E: Same issue as D — this misidentifies which reagent is in excess; it's NaOH, not Al(NO3)3.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0238$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Hydrates and Water of Crystallization$q$, $q$Determine the number of water molecules in a barium hydrate salt from mass-loss data and molar mass.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "sdx4xa", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 75, $q$medium$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is far larger than the real ratio of moles of water to moles of anhydrous salt (≈2) — consistent with a major error in one of the mole conversions.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This overstates the water-to-salt mole ratio — likely from a slip converting either mass to moles.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Still too high compared to the correct ratio of 2 — a smaller-scale version of the same conversion slip.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "This undercounts — dividing the mole amounts the other way round, or a rounding slip, understates the true ratio of about 2.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$2.5 g of a hydrated barium salt gave on heating, 2.13 g of the anhydrous salt. Given that the relative molecular mass of the anhydrous salt is 208, the number of molecules of water of crystallization of the barium salt is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Same approach as any water-of-crystallization question: find the mass of water lost, convert both amounts to moles, then divide. Water lost = 2.5 - 2.13 = 0.37g → 0.37/18 ≈ 0.0206 mol. Moles of anhydrous salt = 2.13/208 ≈ 0.0102 mol. Ratio = 0.0206/0.0102 ≈ 2. Two water molecules per formula unit.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is far larger than the real ratio of moles of water to moles of anhydrous salt (≈2) — consistent with a major error in one of the mole conversions. B: This overstates the water-to-salt mole ratio — likely from a slip converting either mass to moles. C: Still too high compared to the correct ratio of 2 — a smaller-scale version of the same conversion slip. E: This undercounts — dividing the mole amounts the other way round, or a rounding slip, understates the true ratio of about 2.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0239$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solubility$q$, $q$Solubility Calculations$q$, $q$Compute the molar solubility of a salt from the mass dissolved in a given volume of water.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "9jaxsa", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 70, $q$medium$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This overstates the solubility — consistent with a slip converting 10cm3 to dm3 (e.g. dividing by too small a number).", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Also overstates the correct value of 2.5 mol dm-3 — a smaller-scale version of the same volume-conversion slip.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This understates the solubility by a factor of roughly 2.5 — consistent with leaving the volume in cm3 rather than converting to dm3 before dividing.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "This understates the solubility even further, consistent with a compounded unit-conversion and division error.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$3.06 g of a sample of potassium trioxochlorate (V) (KClO3) was required to make a saturated solution with 10cm3 of water at 25°C. The solubility of the salt at 25°C is [K=39, Cl=35.5, O=16]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "5.0 moles dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "3.0 moles dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2.5 moles dm-3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "1.0 moles dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "0.5 moles dm-3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Molar mass of KClO3 = 39 + 35.5 + 48 = 122.5. Moles = 3.06/122.5 = 0.025 mol, dissolved in 10cm3 = 0.01 dm3. Solubility in mol/dm3 is just moles per dm3 of solvent: 0.025/0.01 = 2.5 mol dm-3. The habit worth building: always convert your volume to dm3 before dividing, since solubility in mol dm-3 needs the volume in dm3, not cm3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This overstates the solubility — consistent with a slip converting 10cm3 to dm3 (e.g. dividing by too small a number). B: Also overstates the correct value of 2.5 mol dm-3 — a smaller-scale version of the same volume-conversion slip. D: This understates the solubility by a factor of roughly 2.5 — consistent with leaving the volume in cm3 rather than converting to dm3 before dividing. E: This understates the solubility even further, consistent with a compounded unit-conversion and division error.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0240$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Petroleum Refining$q$, $q$State the industrial purpose of the cracking process.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "yxuq7s", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 30, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Cracking's purpose is to change the SIZE of hydrocarbon chains, not to purify the mixture — purity isn't the driving reason for the process.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Cracking breaks heavy fractions DOWN into lighter ones, moving away from the lubricant/heavy-oil range, not toward more of it.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Asphalt is one of the heaviest fractions — cracking reduces heavy fractions rather than producing more of them.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Candlewax is also a heavier fraction — cracking converts heavy fractions into lighter, more useful ones, not the reverse.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The cracking process is very important in the petroleum industry because it$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "gives purer products", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "yields more lubricants", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "yields more engine fuels", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "yields more asphalt", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "yields more candlewax", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Cracking breaks large, less-useful hydrocarbon chains (the heavy fractions) into smaller ones — exactly the size range that's usable as petrol and other engine fuels. That's the economic reason refineries do it: it converts low-demand heavy fractions into high-demand fuel.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Cracking's purpose is to change the SIZE of hydrocarbon chains, not to purify the mixture — purity isn't the driving reason for the process. B: Cracking breaks heavy fractions DOWN into lighter ones, moving away from the lubricant/heavy-oil range, not toward more of it. D: Asphalt is one of the heaviest fractions — cracking reduces heavy fractions rather than producing more of them. E: Candlewax is also a heavier fraction — cracking converts heavy fractions into lighter, more useful ones, not the reverse.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0241$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Oxidation & Reduction$q$, $q$Oxidizing and Reducing Agents$q$, $q$Recognize a gas that acts as a reducing agent toward one substance and an oxidizing agent toward another.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "0wt48x", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 70, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "O2 is essentially always an oxidizing agent — it doesn't get oxidized further by chlorine to act as a reducing agent, so it fails the first half of the description.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "NO can act as a reducing or oxidizing agent in some contexts, but it isn't the gas matching BOTH specific roles described (reducing toward chlorine and oxidizing toward H2S) — a plausible near-miss.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "NH3 doesn't fit the oxidizing-toward-H2S half of the description — it's not known for oxidizing hydrogen sulphide.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "CO2 is already fully oxidized carbon — it has no further oxidation state to lose, so it can't act as a reducing agent toward chlorine.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A gas that can behave as a reducing agent towards chlorine and as an oxidizing agent towards hydrogen sulphide is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "O2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "NO", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "SO2", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "NH3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "CO2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$This is testing whether you know that the same substance can play different roles depending on what it's reacting with. SO2 reacting with chlorine gets itself oxidized (it's the reducing agent — SO2 + Cl2 + 2H2O → H2SO4 + 2HCl). SO2 reacting with H2S does the opposite: it oxidizes the H2S down to sulphur while SO2 itself gets reduced. Same gas, opposite jobs, depending on the partner.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: O2 is essentially always an oxidizing agent — it doesn't get oxidized further by chlorine to act as a reducing agent, so it fails the first half of the description. B: NO can act as a reducing or oxidizing agent in some contexts, but it isn't the gas matching BOTH specific roles described (reducing toward chlorine and oxidizing toward H2S) — a plausible near-miss. D: NH3 doesn't fit the oxidizing-toward-H2S half of the description — it's not known for oxidizing hydrogen sulphide. E: CO2 is already fully oxidized carbon — it has no further oxidation state to lose, so it can't act as a reducing agent toward chlorine.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0242$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Qualitative Analysis of Ions$q$, $q$Identify a solution from a combined precipitate test and flame test.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kv2dlm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 65, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Na2SO4 does give the white BaCl2 precipitate (it's a sulphate), but sodium's flame colour is yellow, not green — it fails the second clue.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "CaSO4 also gives the white BaCl2 precipitate, but calcium's flame colour is brick-red, not green — it fails the second clue.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "CaCl2 is not a sulphate at all, so it wouldn't give the white BaCl2 precipitate in the first place — it fails the first clue immediately.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "(NH4)2SO4 gives the white BaCl2 precipitate, but ammonium ion has no distinctive strong flame colour like copper's green — it fails the second clue.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following solutions will give a white precipitate with barium chloride solution and a green flame test?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Na2SO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CuSO4", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "CaSO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "CaCl2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "(NH4)2SO4", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$You need both signals to land on one answer. The white precipitate with BaCl2 tells you it's a sulphate — that rules out CaCl2 immediately. The green flame test points to copper, which rules out Na2SO4 (yellow), CaSO4 (brick-red), and (NH4)2SO4 (no strong colour). Only CuSO4 satisfies both the sulphate test and the copper flame colour at once.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Na2SO4 does give the white BaCl2 precipitate (it's a sulphate), but sodium's flame colour is yellow, not green — it fails the second clue. C: CaSO4 also gives the white BaCl2 precipitate, but calcium's flame colour is brick-red, not green — it fails the second clue. D: CaCl2 is not a sulphate at all, so it wouldn't give the white BaCl2 precipitate in the first place — it fails the first clue immediately. E: (NH4)2SO4 gives the white BaCl2 precipitate, but ammonium ion has no distinctive strong flame colour like copper's green — it fails the second clue.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0243$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Atomic Mass$q$, $q$State what determines an atom's mass.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "mzf4b6", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ionization potential is an energy property related to removing an electron, not a measure of atomic mass.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Electrochemical potential is unrelated to how much mass an atom has — a plausible-sounding but irrelevant property.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The number of protons alone gives the atomic NUMBER, which identifies the element — it leaves out neutrons, which also contribute significant mass.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "E", "explanation": "Electrons contribute negligible mass compared to protons and neutrons — pairing neutrons with electrons instead of protons misses where most of the mass actually is.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The mass of an atom is determined by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "its ionization potential", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "its electrochemical potential", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "the number of protons", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "the number of neutrons and protons", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "E", "text": "the number of neutrons and electrons", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Electrons are so light they barely register in the atom's mass — practically all of it sits in the nucleus, in the protons and neutrons together. Protons alone would give you atomic number, not mass.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ionization potential is an energy property related to removing an electron, not a measure of atomic mass. B: Electrochemical potential is unrelated to how much mass an atom has — a plausible-sounding but irrelevant property. C: The number of protons alone gives the atomic NUMBER, which identifies the element — it leaves out neutrons, which also contribute significant mass. E: Electrons contribute negligible mass compared to protons and neutrons — pairing neutrons with electrons instead of protons misses where most of the mass actually is.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0244$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Neutralization Reactions$q$, $q$Identify which of five reagent-mixing scenarios is a genuine acid-base neutralization.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "qe23qr", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 55, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This describes adding a single salt solution on its own — there's no acid-base pair reacting at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Mixing an acid with distilled water is simple dilution, not neutralization — there's no base present to react with the acid.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Mixing two different acids together doesn't neutralize either of them — neutralization specifically requires an acid AND a base.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This describes adding a single salt solution on its own, with no acid or base reacting — not a neutralization at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 1983, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following is a neutralization reaction? A) Addition of chloride solution; B) Addition of trioxonitrate (V) acid (nitric acid) to distilled water; C) Addition of trioxonitrate (V) acid (nitric acid) to tetraoxosulphate (VI) acid (sulphuric acid); D) Addition of trioxonitrate (V) (potassium nitrate) solution; E) Addition of trioxonitrate (V) acid (nitric acid) to potassium hydroxide solution$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Addition of chloride solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Addition of nitric acid to distilled water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Addition of nitric acid to sulphuric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Addition of potassium nitrate solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "E", "text": "Addition of nitric acid to potassium hydroxide solution", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$E$q$, $q$Neutralization needs exactly one acid and one base reacting together. Nitric acid meeting distilled water is just dilution — no base involved. Nitric acid meeting sulphuric acid is two acids together, nothing to neutralize. Only option E pairs an acid (HNO3) with a base (KOH), which is the actual definition of neutralization.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This describes adding a single salt solution on its own — there's no acid-base pair reacting at all. B: Mixing an acid with distilled water is simple dilution, not neutralization — there's no base present to react with the acid. C: Mixing two different acids together doesn't neutralize either of them — neutralization specifically requires an acid AND a base. D: This describes adding a single salt solution on its own, with no acid or base reacting — not a neutralization at all.$q$);
